     "type": "short",
     "probability": "high",
     "answer": "Artists in the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries personified the nation so that the abstract idea of a nation could be given a concrete form; the female figure chosen did not stand for any particular woman in real life. In France the figure was named Marianne, a popular Christian name which underlined the idea of a people's nation. Her characteristics were drawn from Liberty and the Republic, marked by the red cap, the tricolour and the cockade, and statues of Marianne were erected in public squares while her images were marked on coins and stamps to remind people of the national symbol of unity. In Germany the allegory was Germania, who wears a crown of oak leaves, because the German oak stands for heroism. Such images helped ordinary people emotionally identify with the nation and turned nationalism into a living symbol."
