     "question": "Read the extract: 'The most serious source of nationalist tension in Europe after 1871 was the area called the Balkans. The spread of the ideas of romantic nationalism made this region very explosive.' Answer: (a) Which empire controlled the Balkans? (b) Why did the Balkan nationalities seek independence? (c) How did the Balkan crisis lead to the First World War?",
     "marks": 4,
     "type": "source-based",
     "probability": "high",
     "answer": "(a) A large part of the Balkans was under the control of the Ottoman Empire, which by the late nineteenth century was disintegrating under internal weakness and nationalist pressure. (b) The inhabitants were broadly known as Slavs, and the spread of romantic nationalism made the peoples of the region identify themselves on the basis of nationality and shared history; each nationality claimed independence using history to prove that they had once been independent, so jealousy and rivalry developed among the Balkan states themselves. (c) The Balkans became an area of intense rivalry among the big European powers, Russia, Germany, England and Austro Hungary, each of which hoped to gain trade, colonies and naval and military strength there. The Balkan Wars of 1912 and 1913 created further tension, and the Sarajevo assassination of June 1914 ignited the First World War, showing how nationalism aligned with imperialism turned Europe into a war zone."
