     "heading": "Rebellion in the Countryside: Awadh and Baba Ramchandra",
     "para": "In Awadh, peasants were led by Baba Ramchandra, a sanyasi who had earlier been to Fiji as an indentured labourer. The movement was against talukdars and landlords who demanded from peasants exorbitantly high rents and a variety of other cesses, and who made peasants perform begar, that is labour without payment. Peasants had to do begar and work at landlords' farms without any payment. As tenants they had no security of tenure and were regularly evicted so that they could acquire no right over the leased land. The peasant movement demanded reduction of revenue, abolition of begar and a social boycott of oppressive landlords. In many places nai dhobi bandhs were organised by panchayats to deprive landlords of the services of even barbers and washermen. In June 1920 Jawaharlal Nehru began going around villages in Awadh, talking to villagers and trying to understand their grievances, and by October the Oudh Kisan Sabha was set up, headed by Jawaharlal Nehru, Baba Ramchandra and a few others, with branches in over three hundred villages within a month. In 1921 the peasant movement developed in forms that the Congress leadership was unhappy with: houses of talukdars and merchants were attacked, bazaars were looted, and grain hoards were taken over, while local leaders told peasants that Gandhi had declared that no taxes were to be paid and land was to be redistributed among the poor.",
