     "heading": "Chauri Chaura, the Swaraj Party and the Road to Civil Disobedience",
     "para": "In February 1922, Mahatma Gandhi decided to withdraw the Non Cooperation Movement because he felt the movement was turning violent in many places, and satyagrahis needed to be properly trained before they would be ready for mass struggles. The withdrawal disappointed many leaders, and within the Congress two trends emerged: Motilal Nehru and Chittaranjan Das formed the Swaraj Party within the Congress to argue for a return to council politics, while young leaders like Jawaharlal Nehru and Subhas Chandra Bose pressed for more radical mass agitation and for full independence. Two factors shaped Indian politics towards the late 1920s: the first was the worldwide economic depression, as agricultural prices began to fall from 1926 and collapsed after 1930, hitting the peasantry hard. The second was the failure of the Simon Commission, set up in 1928 to look into the functioning of the constitutional system in India and suggest changes, but which did not have a single Indian member, and was therefore greeted with the slogan Go back Simon in all parties. In October 1929 Lord Irwin made a vague offer of dominion status for India and a Round Table Conference, which did not satisfy Congress leaders. In December 1929, under the presidency of Jawaharlal Nehru, the Lahore Congress formalised the demand of Purna Swaraj or full independence for India, declaring that 26 January 1930 would be celebrated as Independence Day, but very few people took part in the celebrations and commitments wavered.",
