     "answer": "The French Revolution (1789-1799) was a period of radical political and social change in France. Its main features included the overthrow of the monarchy, the rise of the bourgeoisie, and the establishment of a republic. The revolution introduced the Declaration of Rights of Man and Citizen, which emphasized liberty, equality, and fraternity. It also abolished feudalism and privileges of the nobility and clergy. The revolution had a profound impact on the world, inspiring nationalist movements and spreading the ideas of democracy and human rights. It led to the rise of Napoleon Bonaparte, whose conquests spread revolutionary ideas across Europe. The revolution also influenced other countries, such as Haiti and Latin America, to seek independence and adopt democratic principles."
